libraries.body:
SAS datasets are referenced as LIBRARY.MEMBER, for example TMP1.FOOD. Read food.sas7bdat directly; no CSV conversion is needed. Assign the library outside the SAS source:

    saslite-custom --lib TMP1=/path/to/data program.sas
    saslite-custom --lib TMP1=. --lib OTHER=../other program.sas

For a persistent project setting, place .saslite.json beside the SAS script:

    {"libraries": {"TMP1": "."}}

Relative config paths resolve against the configuration folder. --lib paths resolve against the terminal's current folder. Repeat --lib for multiple libraries. CLI assignments override project configuration; both override paths in matching LIBNAME statements, including Windows paths. Only the script-adjacent config is loaded. Interactive/-e sessions use the current folder's config. WORK is reserved and cannot be remapped.

The original SAS code can then remain unchanged:

    proc print data=tmp1.food;
    run;

Or assign a library in SAS when desired:

    libname tmp1 "/path/to/data";
    proc print data=tmp1.food; run;

Unmapped relative LIBNAME paths resolve against the SAS script directory. Member names and .sas7bdat extensions are matched case-insensitively. Native files take precedence over same-name XPT files; a corrupt native file causes an error. Native SAS date/time numbers are retained. User-defined format catalogs are not fully supported.

files.body:
Supported library input: native .sas7bdat and SAS Transport .xpt. WORK stores temporary datasets in memory. DATA steps can read native data into WORK:

    data modif;
        set tmp1.comptant;
        if _n_=1 then offre=180;
    run;

Native SAS7BDAT writing is unsupported and raises an error. Configured libraries and LIBNAME persistence write actual .xpt files. They cannot replace a native member with the same name. Explicitly assign DISK for persistent transport output:

    saslite-custom --workdir ./saved --format xpt program.sas

In the program, write DATA DISK.RESULT; ... RUN;. --workdir assigns DISK, not WORK. Its default --format sas7bdat supports reading only; select xpt for writes.

Import/export CSV when you explicitly need it:

    proc import datafile="input.csv" out=demo dbms=csv getnames=yes;
    run;
    proc export data=demo outfile="output.csv" dbms=csv;
    run;

Use absolute paths for import/export when launching outside the data folder. Source text defaults to UTF-8; for older accented SAS scripts:

    saslite-custom --encoding latin1 program.sas
    saslite-custom --encoding cp1252 program.sas

--encoding applies to SAS source text, not the dataset encoding. Native data encoding is read from file metadata.

plots.body:
TTEST and NPAR1WAY plots print automatically as monospaced text. No browser or image terminal is required. Exact values remain in the statistical tables.

    saslite-custom --plot-width 120 program.sas
    saslite-custom --no-plots program.sas

Width range: 40-160 columns. Default uses LINESIZE (80 by default) and available terminal width; --plot-width overrides it. Tables can be wider than plots.

SAS controls:

    ods graphics off;
    ods graphics on;

TTEST selections: ALL, NONE, SUMMARY, HISTOGRAM, BOX, QQPLOT, PROFILES, AGREEMENT and local INTERVAL. Example: PLOTS=(SUMMARY QQPLOT). SUMMARY includes density histograms, boxes and confidence intervals. Paired designs also support profiles and agreement plots.

NPAR1WAY selections: ALL, NONE, WILCOXONBOXPLOT (WILCOXON), FPBOXPLOT (FP), and local BOXPLOT for raw data. Example: PLOTS=(WILCOXONBOXPLOT FPBOXPLOT). Request the corresponding WILCOXON/FP tests. Default boxes show Wilcoxon ranks or FP placements.

Legends: o outlier/observation, * overlapping observations, M mean; boxes span Q1-Q3 with a median line. Histograms use # bars, . normal density and ~ kernel density. Graphs are text adaptations; density bandwidth/binning and rasterization can differ from SAS ODS. SGPLOT adds the explicit graph commands described below.

PNG export: an explicit ODS GRAPHICS ON enables PNG files in addition to text plots. Until then, existing TTEST/NPAR1WAY programs remain text-only. SGPLOT requires PNG export to be enabled. ODS GRAPHICS OFF suppresses both renderers in SASLite, including SGPLOT. --no-plots overrides ODS ON. NOPRINT and PLOTS=NONE suppress both formats for TTEST/NPAR1WAY.

    ods listing gpath="./figures";
    ods graphics on / imagename="intro" imagefmt=png;
    proc sgplot data=sample; histogram age; run;
    ods graphics off;

Without GPATH, PNGs go to graphs/ beside the top-level .sas file. Inline code, interactive sessions and GUI code without a source filename use the working directory. Relative GPATH is resolved against that same base when the ODS statement executes. Included files share the top-level script base. No folder is created until an image is successfully rendered.

IMAGENAME sets a filename base; otherwise names identify the dataset, plot type, variable/pair and BY group. Existing files are preserved with numbered suffixes, even across runs. Every saved absolute path is logged and available through result.image_paths. Options-only ODS GRAPHICS / IMAGENAME="intro" IMAGEFMT=PNG; configures output without enabling it. Only PNG, IMAGENAME and GPATH are supported: image dimensions, styles, other formats and full ODS destinations are not implemented.

Images are 1200 by 800 pixels, rendered locally with Matplotlib Agg without a display server. Each SGPLOT block writes one PNG. TTEST writes one per selected panel type and variable/pair/BY group; NPAR1WAY writes raw/rank/placement boxes as requested. Histograms, normal/kernel fits, SAS-style box quartiles, Q-Q positions, mean intervals and paired diagnostics use shared numerical helpers. PNG histogram binning uses the existing rule at nominal width 80; --plot-width only controls text rendering. One-sided confidence intervals use arrows on their unbounded side. Rendering is an approximation of SAS graphics, not a pixel-identical ODS implementation.

interactive.body:
Start an interactive session with either command:

    saslite-custom
    saslite-custom -i

Enter each complete DATA/PROC step on one line; the current prompt submits when it sees a semicolon. For multiline programs, save a .sas file and run it. WORK data persist for the life of the interactive session.

    sas> proc print data=tmp1.food; run;
    sas> help;
    sas> help fp;
    sas> exit;

help and help TOPIC also work without the final semicolon. Help does not clear your pending input or execute SAS. quit; and exit; leave the prompt. Ctrl+C clears pending input; Ctrl+D exits. Ordinary file runs need no restart. Restart an existing Python/interactive session after upgrading the package.

language.body:
The interpreter supports a subset of DATA-step and SQL syntax. Common DATA-step features include INPUT/DATALINES, SET, MERGE, assignments, IF/THEN/ELSE, WHERE, DO loops, OUTPUT, DELETE, RETAIN, KEEP/DROP/RENAME, LENGTH, LABEL and FORMAT. List variables explicitly when shorthand ranges are not accepted.

    data sample;
        input group value;
        datalines;
    1 10
    1 12
    2 18
    2 22
    ;
    run;

    data changed;
        set sample;
        doubled=value*2;
    run;

PROC SQL supports SELECT and table operations, filtering, joins, grouping and aggregate functions. Macro support includes %LET, %PUT, %MACRO/%MEND and %INCLUDE with practical limitations.

    %let response=value;
    proc means data=sample; var &response; run;

Use help procedures for procedure examples and help functions for the installed function list. Parsing a SAS statement does not guarantee every option has full SAS behavior.

by-groups.body:
MEANS, SUMMARY, TTEST and NPAR1WAY support multiple BY variables, DESCENDING before each descending variable, and trailing NOTSORTED. Ordinary BY requires correctly sorted observations: use PROC SORT with the same BY variables and directions. NOTSORTED processes contiguous groups in input order; a repeated key after another group produces a separate analysis. Missing BY values form groups (first in ascending order), and numeric BY variables are excluded from automatic analysis-variable selection.

    proc sort data=sample; by site; run;
    proc ttest data=sample sides=u; by site; var value; run;

CLASS operates inside each BY group. Tables, graphs, filenames and diagnostics identify the group. A failed inference group makes the procedure unsuccessful but other valid groups still run. WEIGHT and FREQ remain unsupported. BY uses unformatted values; format-based grouping and special SAS missing-value classes are not implemented.

PRINT.body:
Print a dataset, optionally selecting variables.

    proc print data=tmp1.food; run;
    proc print data=tmp1.food; var Couleur Desir; run;

MEANS.body:
Statistics: N, NMISS, MEAN, SUM, MIN, MAX, STD and MEDIAN. Default display: N, MEAN, STD, MIN, MAX. Supports VAR, CLASS, BY, OUTPUT, NOPRINT/PRINT, MAXDEC=0..20, NWAY and MISSING. Unknown variables/statistics and nonnumeric analysis variables are errors. Missing numeric observations are excluded independently for each variable; N counts nonmissing values and NMISS counts missing values. Without VAR, MEANS selects numeric variables excluding BY/CLASS (or counts observations if none remain).

    proc means data=sample n nmiss mean maxdec=2;
        by site; class group; var age visits;
        output out=summary mean=mean_age mean_visits n=n_age n_visits;
    run;

    proc means data=sample nway missing;
        class group; var age visits;
        output out=summary mean= n= / autoname;
    run;

OUTPUT maps names in VAR order. Partial name lists save only the corresponding first variables; statistic-specific lists such as MEAN(visits)=mean_visits select a subset. With no names, variables retain their names; / AUTONAME generates VARIABLE_STAT names and resolves generated conflicts. Explicit conflicts are errors. Multiple OUTPUT statements are supported; an omitted OUT= generates an unused WORK.DATAn. The local procedure-level OUT= extension behaves as OUTPUT OUT= without explicit statistics.

CLASS combinations are summarized independently within BY groups. Output includes _TYPE_ (class-combination bitmask) and _FREQ_ (observations in that group). Without NWAY, output includes all observed class combinations and the overall summary; NWAY keeps full combinations only. Printed output shows full combinations. Missing CLASS observations are excluded by default; MISSING includes a missing class level. With no explicit output statistics, five rows per combination are saved, identified by _STAT_=N, MIN, MAX, MEAN, STD. All-missing statistics display a dot; STD needs at least two observations. Data source rows are never modified. Weights, frequencies, TYPES/WAYS, IDGROUP, output dataset options, formatted classes and additional statistics remain unsupported.

SUMMARY.body:
Shares MEANS calculations, grouping and OUTPUT syntax, but suppresses display by default. PRINT enables display and NOPRINT suppresses it. Without VAR, SUMMARY counts observations and saves _TYPE_/_FREQ_ without analysis statistics. Explicit requested statistics require VAR.

    proc summary data=sample nway;
        by site; class group; var age visits;
        output out=summary mean= / autoname;
    run;

See help means and help by-groups.

TTEST.body:
Options: DATA=, H0= (default 0), ALPHA= (default .05), NOPRINT, PLOTS=. Normal analysis; SIDES=2 (default), L (alternative mean/difference < H0), U (alternative > H0). Signed finite H0 values are supported. SIDES controls p-values and mean confidence limits; SD limits and Folded F remain unchanged. Shows N, mean, sample Std Dev, Std Err, min/max, mean/SD confidence limits, pooled and Satterthwaite tests and Folded F when applicable.

    proc ttest data=tmp1.food;
        class Couleur; var Desir;
    run;

    proc ttest data=tmp1.food h0=50 alpha=.05;
        var Desir;
    run;

    proc ttest data=paired_data;
        paired before*after;
    run;

CLASS must identify two groups. Without CLASS/PAIRED, VAR selects one-sample tests. PAIRED tests differences using complete pairs. If VAR is omitted, eligible numeric variables are selected (excluding CLASS and BY). See help plots for graph controls. BY, DESCENDING and NOTSORTED are supported; see help by-groups. WEIGHT, FREQ, ratio tests and exact tests are unsupported.

    proc ttest data=sample sides=u h0=-1 alpha=.10;
        by site; var value;
    run;

NPAR1WAY.body:
Request WILCOXON (Mann-Whitney-Wilcoxon), FP (Fligner-Policello), or both. MWW is a local alias for WILCOXON. DATA= and one CLASS variable are required. VAR may list multiple responses.

    proc npar1way data=tmp1.food wilcoxon fp;
        class Couleur;
        var Desir;
    run;

MWW: rank-sum S, Mann-Whitney U for the identified group, normal and t approximations, tie adjustments and Kruskal-Wallis. CORRECT=NO disables its default continuity correction. With more than two groups WILCOXON reports Kruskal-Wallis.

FP: placement N/sum/mean/Std Dev, placement-sum difference, Z and normal one-/two-sided p-values. Ties get half credit. Exactly two groups with at least two observations each are required. FP assumes within-group symmetry and does not apply the MWW continuity correction.

    proc npar1way data=tmp1.food fp(refclass=1);
        class Couleur; var Desir;
    run;

FP defaults to first-listed minus second-listed class; listing order follows input occurrence. REFCLASS=1/2 selects a position. A quoted value, e.g. FP(REFCLASS='2'), selects that class label. Reversing reference changes the sign, not the two-sided p-value.

NOPRINT suppresses output. PLOTS=NONE suppresses graphs; PLOTS=(WILCOXONBOXPLOT FPBOXPLOT) selects rank/placement boxes. BY, DESCENDING and NOTSORTED are supported; see help by-groups. Automatic analysis-variable selection excludes CLASS and BY. Exact tests, WEIGHT, FREQ and other NPAR1WAY tests are unsupported.

CORR.body:
Pearson (default), Spearman or Kendall correlations; VAR selects numeric variables. NOSIMPLE hides summaries; OUTP= stores the matrix. Current calculations use complete cases across selected variables.

    proc corr data=tmp1.food spearman;
        var Attraction Desir Plaisir;
    run;

REG.body:
MODEL specifies response and predictors. Supports NOINT, OUTEST= and OUTPUT predictions/residuals.

    proc reg data=tmp1.food;
        model Desir = Attraction Plaisir;
    run;

LOGISTIC.body:
MODEL selects the binary response and predictors. EVENT=, CLASS, ODDSRATIO, ALPHA= and output estimates are available as a subset of SAS logistic syntax.

    proc logistic data=study;
        model outcome(event='1') = age;
    run;

FREQ.body:
One-way tables and cross-tabulations using TABLES; options include MISSING, NOPRINT, NOROW, NOCOL and NOPERCENT. Not all SAS test/options syntax is supported.

    proc freq data=tmp1.food;
        tables Couleur;
    run;

SQL.body:
SELECT, filtering, joins, grouping and aggregate functions; CREATE TABLE, INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE are supported subsets. End with QUIT.

    proc sql;
        select Couleur, avg(Desir) as MeanDesir
        from tmp1.food group by Couleur;
    quit;

IMPORT.body:
DATAFILE=, OUT=, DBMS=, GETNAMES= and DELIMITER= are supported. Native SAS files use libraries instead of CSV conversion.

    proc import datafile="input.csv" out=demo dbms=csv getnames=yes;
    run;

TABULATE.body:
CLASS, VAR and basic TABLE expressions produce summary tables; complex SAS layouts are outside this subset.

    proc tabulate data=tmp1.food;
        class Couleur; var Desir; table Couleur, Desir;
    run;

REPORT.body:
COLUMN/COLUMNS, DEFINE and BY provide basic reporting. Full SAS report computation blocks and styling are unsupported.

    proc report data=tmp1.food nowd;
        column Couleur Desir;
        define Couleur / group;
        define Desir / mean;
    run;

SGPLOT.body:
Requires DATA= and exactly one of HISTOGRAM variable;, HBOX variable;, or SCATTER X=variable Y=variable; per RUN. Variables must be numeric. Missing/nonfinite plot observations are omitted (complete X/Y pairs for scatter); the omitted count is reported. No usable observations or unsupported syntax is an error. Source datasets are unchanged.

    proc sgplot data=intro_ex1; histogram age; run;

    proc sgplot data=intro_ex1; hbox age; run;

    proc sgplot data=intro_ex1; scatter x=age y=n_visite; run;

Enable saving with ODS GRAPHICS ON before the procedure. Histograms display percent, HBOX uses SAS-style quartiles with 1.5-IQR whiskers and outliers, and scatter displays the valid pairs. Available dataset labels are used on axes, including accented text. See help plots and examples/graphics.sas. Plot options, overlays, SGPLOT BY/WHERE statements, TITLE/axis statements and other graph types are outside this initial subset. A SAS statement comment begins with * and ends with a semicolon; use *ods rtf; or /* ods rtf */ to comment out a statement.

DATA-step LABEL statements now preserve labels correctly for metadata and plotted axes.
